<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limes Sorabicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Limes Sorabicus was the eastern frontier zone of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> facing the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorbs">Sorbs</a>, running roughly along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in present-day central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Set up after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s campaigns against the Sorbs in the early 9th century, it was a line of fortified places and border districts under Frankish counts rather than a continuous wall. The Ottonians pushed the frontier further east in the 10th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limes-sorabicus.jpg" alt="Limes Sorabicus" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: prof. Włodzimierz Dzwonkowski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slavs_west_territory_Limes_Sorabicus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
